// @test: chained method call on a Vec returned by a by-value-struct-argument call (#412)
// @description: a method call chained directly onto a call whose argument is a by-value
//   struct compiled to nothing at all -- `f(x)` was evaluated in LHS ("pointer") mode,
//   which pushed the receiver's pointer semantics down into the call's own argument
//   evaluation, so `x` reached `f` as its address ("Argument type mismatch for call to
//   f. Expected Big but got ptr") and the statement was dropped: `n` was never assigned
//   and the program printed nothing but compile diagnostics. A call-result receiver has
//   no storage slot, so it is now evaluated normally and the returned Vec is materialized
//   into its own temporary. Binding the result first (`w = f(x); w.len()`) always worked
//   and is asserted alongside the chained form.
// @category: ownership, vec, struct
// @expect-output: chained len=5
// @expect-return: 0

share(all)
struct Big {
    a<Vec<Int>>,
    b<Int>
}

share(all)
f(b<Big>)<Vec<Int>> -> {
    return b.a
}

main()<Int> -> {
    v<Vec<Int>> = Vec()
    for (i in 0..4) { v.push(i) }
    x<Big> = Big { a = v, b = 1 }

    w<Vec<Int>> = f(x)
    println("bound len=" + w.len().to_string())

    n<Int> = f(x).len()
    println("chained len=" + n.to_string())
    return 0
}
